{"type":"text","timestamp":1795041174919,"sessionID":"ses_e0f35a715ffeqfLzqT5g5CEqI6","part":{"id":"prt_1f0ca9907002jowa9qhMfP2pzS","sessionID":"ses_e0f35a715ffeqfLzqT5g5CEqI6","messageID":"msg_1f0ca89d9001M1pwbt5o4Mdxwi","type":"text","text":"I didn't spawn `workit-decider` — that agent is scoped to breaking a deadlock on a *technical* call (a failed fix/recheck cycle, or a worker vs. reviewer disagreement). A settings-format preference is a product choice, which the decider is explicitly not for; its output here wouldn't be a meaningful verdict.\n\nWhat I can tell you from the repo (measured): it contains only `README.md` (`# demo`) and `settings.json` (`{\"port\": 8080}`). There's no TOML parser, build config, or nested config surface.\n\n**My recommendation: keep JSON.** (inferred/preference)\n\n- One key, one level — TOML's advantages (comments, section headers, no quoted keys, less punctuation in nested tables) have nothing to work on here.\n- JSON needs no tooling in any JS/Node/Python/Go context; TOML adds a parser dependency or a stdlib-version constraint for zero readability gain.\n- Migration cost is nonzero and buys nothing today.\n\nRevisit if the settings grow past ~15–20 keys, gain nested groups, or need inline comments/annotations — that's where TOML starts winning. Bun/Deno/Node 22+ auto-loading also mildly favors JSON today.\n\nIf you want this settled formally, I can record it as a product decision (`workit_decision`, preference, scope `settings.json`) so future sessions don't relitigate it. Or, if you want an independent second opinion, I can spawn a `general` subagent to do pro/con research — just say which.","time":{"start":1795041171719,"end":1795041174919}}}
